hello and welcome to the daily climate  show coming up  homes with electric cars and heat pumps  are saving more than a thousand pounds a  year as inflation continues to rise  europe's most powerful electric charging  hub has opened in oxford with the  capacity to recharge a car in 20 minutes  and a year's worth of rain in three days  around sydney leaves 50 000 people  facing evacuation from their homes  hello and welcome to the daily climate  show where we track the changes  happening to our planet hear from the  people hit hardest by those changes and  reports on the race for solutions now we  start the show with an exclusive about  the amount of money being saved by  households using electric cars and heat  pumps and the good news is you'll be  making big savings compared to  households using petrol or diesel or gas  spoilers so let's take a look at the  numbers for you now the research by the  energy and climate intelligence unit has  found that households with fossil fuel  cars and gas boilers are spending 1180  pounds more each year than their  electric counterparts and with inflation  uh that's almost doubled from 620  from last year and this extra spending  is expected to rise to 1 600 pounds in  october and when the energy price cap  rises again well joining me now from the  scottish highlands to talk through this  is simon crane mcgreen head of analysts  at the eciu simon thanks so much for  joining us my first question is what do  you make of those savings  thanks for having me  these savings illustrate what households  are painfully aware of which is that  over the past year the price of fossil  fuels has soared and this has had a huge  knock-on effect on on bills  and the only real way to avoid the pain  of fossil fuel prices is to stop using  them whether house households or  nationally  and as you pointed out there an electric  household with a heat pump an electric  vehicle and better insulation was  already saving over 600 pounds a year on  energy bills last year  and the office of national statistics  shows that as of may this year that's  risen to almost 1200 pounds and our  assessment is it'll rise even further to  around sixteen hundred pounds in your  saving but what's the october price cap  simon you make very good points there i  suppose the question of those upfront  costs and i'm talking about perhaps heat  pumps that can start at around 7 000  pounds for low income households that's  a big chunk of money isn't it  that's absolutely true and  if we think about heat pumps the  government currently has a grant the  boiler upgrade scheme and it can bring  the cost of a heat pump insulation down  to around the same price as a gas boiler  so households who are thinking of having  to replace a gas boiler anyway for  whatever reason now have that option of  a heat pump which is now viable and the  government's working with industry to  bring the actual base cost of the heat  pump down to around the same as a gas  boiler by the end of the decade to make  it even more accessible  and also there are many uh easier  options as well that people can consider  insulation is a really important way of  reducing your gas bills  and there are millions of homes in the  uk that don't have topped up loft  insulation that's a very easy thing to  do  and don't have cow to wall insulation  and these each cost a few hundred pounds  and again for some people that's too  much at the moment  but that's where schemes such as eco the  energy company obligation comes in  it's a scheme uh funded through uh bills  which the government  helps around was more than actually 100  000 homes a year to get better heat  insulation and heating systems and that  can save an average of 600 pounds a year  for poor households under the current  gas prices  simon what is your advice there you've  made some points about insulation and we  did that on the the weekend climate show  with tom heap and he went to visit  someone who was  installing insulation but what advice do  you have for people watching at home  that are thinking about that energy bill  that's coming in what advice can they do  how can they make their home more energy  efficient without breaking the bank  well as i say there are some simple  insulation measures that can still be  done to a lot of homes loft insulation  cavity wall insulation draft exclusion  simple measures um that are relatively  cheap for those who can afford and for  those who are  really struggling and can't afford the  upfront cost then  look into what your energy supplier  can offer through the eco scheme or  possibly through the borrower upgrade  scheme  okay simon cram agree and thank you very  much for joining us on the show today  well let's take a look at today's other  climate news now and climate protesters  have struck for the fifth time in a week  at a major art gallery sticking  themselves to the frame of a copy of  leonardo da vinci's the last supper five  just stop oil supporters attached  themselves to the frame at the royal  academy in central london it comes as  six people believed to be from the same  group were charged over track invasion  at the beginning of the british grand  prix at silverstein  an energy super hub opens today in  oxford designed to decarbonize the city  by providing rapid charging for dozens  of electric vehicles at any one time  now the site combines europe's most  powerful ev charging hub with the  world's largest hybrid battery and is  powered by renewable energy now the  project is part of a planned nationwide  network of super hubs focusing on  reducing carbon emissions from transport  to support uk's clean energy transition  meanwhile sales of battery electric  vehicles in the uk have grown by more  than 14  in a month but continuing supply chain  shortages are affecting the production  of plug-in hybrid vehicles with sales  falling by more than a third there  the queen's property manager has mapped  five areas of the coast off cornwall and  wales which could be developed for  offshore wind projects now the crown  estate identified areas with deeper  water and higher winds which are  suitable for floating rather than the  traditional wind farms now the plan is  to deliver four gigawatts of offshore uh  floating wind power to almost four  million homes by 2035.  and gardeners are being urged to plant  with climate change in mind the royal  horticultural society's planet-friendly  garden highlights solutions to help the  climate crisis including allowing lawns  to go brown in summer rather than  watering and planting trees and hedges  to help store carbon  now as many as 50 000 people are facing  evacuation as severe rain continues to  fall around the australian city of  sydney experts say climate change has  made the rain much worse after years  worth of rain fell in three days in some  regions our correspondent nicole johnson  is in the town of windsor near sydney  and she sent this update  a rescue on the outskirts of sydney this  family believed they could handle the  flood but the water rose so fast before  they knew it the only way out was by  boat waking up at about 2am and seeing  water creeping up to the house it was  it was difficult sleeping but uh yeah no  it's it's definitely scary especially  with kids  already this year there's been multiple  floods in new south wales  and people are tired of it flood fatigue  there is coveting this flood fatigue at  the moment with me  that's what i'm feeling the the two  words to describe it it's just  i'm just tired of the floods more than  30 000 people have been evacuated from  homes in and around sydney  or have been warned to get ready for it  as yet again australia's east coast is  battered by rain  a month's worth of it in only two days  it's incredible uh in the last well 18  months we've had three major flood  events in this area and we're talking  about water depths getting way above  head level um in some areas and you know  multiple people having to be evacuated  animals needing to be rescued and also  people as well  wild weather saw a cargo ship stuck  without power  and drifting towards rocky sea cliffs  a helicopter rescue had to be aborted  eventually it was towed away from danger  this major bridge on the outskirts of  sydney has been lost under a mile wide  river and with the flood waters still  rising people are bracing themselves for  even more homes to go under  so they're getting ready  moving whatever they can to higher  ground  or a shipping container  this is an area prone to flooding  but not this often  keith levy moved in here six months ago  since then  four floods  the ground floor is under a metre of  muddy water  he's given up bothering to pump it out  is this climate change  i think partially i think there's a lot  of factors i think damn management as  well is  the cause of what's also going on and  also the human footprint we're putting  on  the area as well  with barely time to recover from one  flood the region is hit with another  it's been called sydney's new normal  but nothing feels normal about this  nicole johnston sky news  sydney  um before we go we want your climate  questions by the way anything that's  ever mystified you or whatever you want  to get to the bottom of you can scan the  qr code on your screen right now and can  send us your questions some of which  will be answered on our new weekend  climate show hosted by the environment  broadcaster and author tom heap and  you'll be able to watch that on saturday  and sunday at 3 30 here on sky news
 

